      "message": "Let\u0027s simplify this code by pushing the guard checks down into `DoUsbTransfer`.\n\nHere\u0027s code generated by Gemini. Review for correctness:\n\n----\n\n### The Rationale\n\nMoving the validation checks and `const` casting directly into `DoUsbTransfer` is a significant architectural improvement over the original patch. Here is why this refactor makes the codebase cleaner and more robust:\n\n1. **DRY (Don\u0027t Repeat Yourself):** In the original patch, the exact same guard clauses (`if (!dev_handle_ptr_)` and `if (buff.empty())`) are duplicated across all four endpoint methods (`IapSendCommand`, `IapRecvStatus`, `IapSendData`, `IapRecvData`). Moving them to the lowest common denominator (`DoUsbTransfer`) completely eliminates this redundant boilerplate.\n2. **Guaranteed Safety:** `DoUsbTransfer` acts as the ultimate choke point for all hardware communication in your class. By placing the checks here, you guarantee that no transfer can ever bypass validation. If a developer adds a new endpoint method in the future, the architecture automatically enforces these safety checks.\n3. **Modernizing the API \u0026 Hiding C-API Quirks:** The original `DoUsbTransfer` relied on legacy C-style `(uint8_t* data, int length)` arguments. Upgrading this signature to accept `std::span` modernizes the lowest layer of your code. Furthermore, because the `libusb` C-API requires a mutable pointer even for strictly read-only OUT transfers, providing an overloaded `DoUsbTransfer` to handle the `const_cast` internally hides this ugly reality from the rest of your C++ class.\n\n---\n\n### Required Changes (Relative to the Original Patch)\n\nHere are the specific changes needed relative to your original code, formatted to strictly adhere to Google C++ Style Guidelines.\n\n#### 1. Update the Header (`iap_control.h`)\n\nReplace the original `DoUsbTransfer` declaration with two overloaded versions that accept spans: one for mutable buffers (IN transfers) and one for const buffers (OUT transfers).\n\n```cpp\n// ... existing private methods ...\n  std::expected\u003cvoid, IapError\u003e IapRecvData(std::span\u003cuint8_t\u003e buff);\n\n  std::expected\u003cvoid, IapError\u003e DoUsbTransfer(\n      uint8_t endpoint, std::span\u003cuint8_t\u003e buff,\n      std::source_location location \u003d std::source_location::current());\n\n  std::expected\u003cvoid, IapError\u003e DoUsbTransfer(\n      uint8_t endpoint, std::span\u003cconst uint8_t\u003e buff,\n      std::source_location location \u003d std::source_location::current());\n// ...\n\n```\n\n#### 2. Update `DoUsbTransfer` Implementations (`iap_control.cc`)\n\nFirst, implement the mutable span version. Move the guard clauses from the endpoint methods to the very top of this function. Update the internal `libusb` call to extract the pointer and size directly from the newly provided span.\n\n```cpp\nstd::expected\u003cvoid, IapError\u003e IapControl::DoUsbTransfer(\n    uint8_t endpoint, std::span\u003cuint8_t\u003e buff,\n    const std::source_location location) {\n  if (!dev_handle_ptr_) {\n    return std::unexpected(IapError::InvalidDeviceHandle);\n  }\n\n  if (buff.empty()) {\n    return std::unexpected(IapError::BufferEmpty);\n  }\n\n  int transferred \u003d 0;\n  int err_code \u003d libusb_interrupt_transfer(\n      dev_handle_ptr_.get(), endpoint, buff.data(),\n      static_cast\u003cint\u003e(buff.size()), \u0026transferred, kTimeoutTransferMs);\n\n  if (err_code !\u003d 0) {\n    LogErr(\"[IAP] {} - fail, error code: {} ({})\\n\", location.function_name(),\n           err_code, libusb_error_name(err_code));\n    return std::unexpected(IapError::TransferFailed);\n  }\n\n  if (transferred !\u003d static_cast\u003cint\u003e(buff.size())) {\n    LogErr(\"[IAP] {} - fail, data transferred {} is less than expected\\n\",\n           location.function_name(), transferred);\n    return std::unexpected(IapError::TransferSizeMismatch);\n  }\n\n  return {};\n}\n\n```\n\nNext, implement the `const` overload right below it. This handles the casting required by `libusb` safely in one place, delegating the actual work to the mutable overload above.\n\n```cpp\nstd::expected\u003cvoid, IapError\u003e IapControl::DoUsbTransfer(\n    uint8_t endpoint, std::span\u003cconst uint8_t\u003e buff,\n    const std::source_location location) {\n  // Safely cast away const for OUT transfers. The libusb C API lacks the \n  // const qualifier, but it does not modify the host buffer during an OUT transfer.\n  return DoUsbTransfer(\n      endpoint,\n      std::span\u003cuint8_t\u003e(const_cast\u003cuint8_t*\u003e(buff.data()), buff.size()),\n      location);\n}\n\n```\n\n#### 3. Simplify the Endpoint Wrappers (`iap_control.cc`)\n\nNow, strip the guard clauses out of the four endpoint functions in your original patch. Because you have function overloading set up, you simply pass the span to `DoUsbTransfer`, and the compiler will route it to the correct implementation automatically.\n\n```cpp\nstd::expected\u003cvoid, IapError\u003e IapControl::IapSendCommand(\n    std::span\u003cconst uint8_t\u003e buff) {\n  return DoUsbTransfer(kEp1Addr, buff);\n}\n\nstd::expected\u003cvoid, IapError\u003e IapControl::IapRecvStatus(\n    std::span\u003cuint8_t\u003e buff) {\n  return DoUsbTransfer(kEp2Addr, buff);\n}\n\nstd::expected\u003cvoid, IapError\u003e IapControl::IapSendData(\n    std::span\u003cconst uint8_t\u003e buff) {\n  return DoUsbTransfer(kEp3Addr, buff);\n}\n\nstd::expected\u003cvoid, IapError\u003e IapControl::IapRecvData(std::span\u003cuint8_t\u003e buff) {\n  return DoUsbTransfer(kEp4Addr, buff);\n}\n\n```",
